#: lib/potok_ide_web/domain_copy.ex:1310
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "For a viewer who may edit its group, an app can make <Group>groups</Group> right under it, change them and take them back, and put them on a map with a pin. A new one opens its chat if the app's group has. It can also make up to a hundred at once, all or none, each with a key of its own, so that the same list brought in again makes no duplicates: that is how the rows of an open dataset become groups on a map. That is how one app writes what another shows, and a feed gets its editors' desk."
msgstr ""

#: lib/potok_ide_web/domain_copy.ex:1301
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "It gets the <Group>groups</Group> right under it that have a place on a map, with the pin each one shows, and whoever may make a sub-group there can add one where they tap. A tap on a pin opens that group's description over the app, where its own app, a form or a poll, works with that group's rights. Among them, groups that cover an area come with their borders, groups that run along a route with their lines and lengths, and each place with the areas it lies in or passes through, so the app draws the areas and routes and shows only the groups inside one area. A map of more than 2000 places gets only what its view shows, which the server gathers into numbered circles as on the root's map, filtered by name and by the pins the groups show. That is how a group becomes a map. The root needs no such app: it has a map of its own."
msgstr ""

#: lib/potok_ide_web/domain_copy.ex:1535
