#: lib/potok_ide_web/domain_copy.ex:756
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "What the data of the processes drawn in a group looks like, as its editors define it: a name and up to 30 attributes, each with the type of what it holds. An attribute holds a text, a number, a yes or no, a date, a date and time, one of the choices it lists, or another entity of the group, and may be a list of them. Entities nest at most four deep and never in a circle. A group has at most 50, each name once in any letter case, and one that another holds, or that a process drawn in the group keeps a variable of, cannot be deleted. Deleting the group takes them along."
msgstr ""

#: lib/potok_ide_web/domain_copy.ex:362
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "what its variables hold"
msgstr ""

#: lib/potok_ide_web/domain_copy.ex:1601
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A <AppProcess>process</AppProcess> drawn in Potok that starts on a call, on a signal, by hand or on a value may take an <GroupEntity>entity</GroupEntity> of its group. A call then hands it that entity's attributes, each a value of its type and every required one a value, checked when the caller is saved and again when the call is made, against the entity as the called process was saved with it; a call that does not fit fails where it stands and starts nothing. A process started by hand asks for the attributes in a form, which is why it takes only an entity of plain attributes. A signal is waited for with an entity, and a process sends it only with the entity those who wait for it expect, where someone does: whoever waits says what it waits for. A drawing whose entity changed since it was saved, or whose calls and signals no longer fit those they talk to, runs on as it was saved, and its editors are told to look it over."
msgstr ""

#: lib/potok_ide_web/domain_copy.ex:1599
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "What a process takes is an entity"
msgstr ""

#: lib/potok_ide_web/domain_copy.ex:320
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "entity it takes"
msgstr ""

#: lib/potok_ide_web/domain_copy.ex:1593
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Besides the process an app writes into its rules, the editors of a group can draw processes of their own in Potok, as <AppProcess>app processes</AppProcess> that keep their graph outside any app's settings. One starts on a new value of a collection of an app, on a member's chat message holding one of its words or mentioning its role, on a sub-group made one at a time (not a record, a thread or what a process makes), on a schedule, when someone holding its role starts it by hand with a title and a text, when another process drawn in the group calls it, or on a signal one sends. An app replaces its settings whole when it saves them, so it could never keep a drawing safe; this way it neither sees nor overwrites them, and its own process runs beside them. A drawing is kept only when an instance could finish it, with roles the group has, and starts switched off. What its texts and conditions say are expressions over the names it has, such as what set it going and what a decision ended with, checked when it is kept, so a drawing that names what is not there is refused. One kept before expressions is turned into them when its editor opens it, and an instance reads the language of the graph it started with. A drawing may also declare variables, each with the type of what it holds, a plain one or an <GroupEntity>entity</GroupEntity> of the group: a step sets one to what an expression gives, every instance keeps its own, and the drawing is kept with the entities as they were then, so an instance under way reads them the same after one changes. A step may also go through a list an item at a time, with steps of its own for each: it reads the list once, on its way in, at most 200 items, and hands each to those steps by a name. So that a drawing that runs in a circle stops, an instance takes at most 25 steps in a row without waiting, counted anew for each item, and 5,000 in its life; after 100 it gives way to the others, and the steps that write outside it (a message, a sub-group, a push, a field of the group, a signal, a call) are 60 a minute for a process at most. Switched on, its profile joins the group and each of these starts a <ValueProcess>process instance</ValueProcess> of it; a change applies to the instances that start after it, and switching it off cancels those that run or wait. The app knows nothing of their tasks, so the people of a task's role finish them on the group's Processes tab, where they see a <Value>value</Value> only as far as they may read it. Deleting the app takes the processes drawn on its collections along."
msgstr ""

#: lib/potok_ide_web/domain_copy.ex:1621
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A drawn <AppProcess>process</AppProcess> that others call is private until an editor makes it public. A private one is called by the processes of its own <Group>group</Group> alone. A public one is called from the groups above its group and from those below it too, never from a branch beside it, and the editors there see its name and what it takes. The <ValueProcess>run</ValueProcess> such a call starts in another group gets what the call hands over and nothing else: it has no subject and nobody set it off, as the caller's value and people are no part of that group, and the caller hears only which end it reached. Callers of other groups do not keep a public process from being deleted or made private again: their calls then fail where they stand, their editors are told to look the drawing over, and a caller left waiting for a run that went with its group fails as one whose run was cancelled."
msgstr ""

#: lib/potok_ide_web/domain_copy.ex:1616
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A process is private to its group, or public up and down the tree"
msgstr ""

#: lib/potok_ide_web/domain_copy.ex:1019
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Only a process drawn in Potok that another calls can be public."
msgstr ""

#: lib/potok_ide_web/domain_copy.ex:1629
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "What a process writes starts no other process: its messages and the sub-groups it makes set nothing going, so two processes never keep each other running by chance. A drawn process talks to another only by a step drawn for it. A call starts a drawn <AppProcess>process</AppProcess> that starts when another calls it, of the same group or a public one of a group above or below, and hands over what it takes; the <ValueProcess>run</ValueProcess> it starts is one level deeper, and in its own group it shares the caller's subject. A caller that waits goes on when that run ends, knowing which end it reached, or gives up at its deadline and cancels it. Cancelling a run cancels the run it waits for, and a run whose called run is cancelled stops, for an editor to take up again, which calls anew. A step can also keep a <GroupField>field of the group</GroupField>, which every drawn process of the group reads; adding to one takes its lock, so two runs never lose each other's count. A signal wakes the runs of the group's drawn processes that wait for it just then, at most 100, and starts the processes that start on it; nothing keeps it for a run that comes to wait later, and a wait may take only a signal sent on its own subject. Runs go at most three processes deep, and a process another one of its group calls cannot be deleted."
msgstr ""

